From 39277ec2c2fb2e92c948bd2372d0c50c5c18004d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EcoWoods Build <build@ecowoods.ca>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 20:16:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(agents): the .md editions carry their sources; the repo
 root stops carrying litter
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three things, one theme: the surfaces built for machines were the ones missing
the thing that makes this site quotable, and the repository root had become a
place where unchecked files accumulate.

1. THE CITATIONS WERE MISSING FROM THE EDITIONS THAT NEED THEM MOST

`Paper.references` shipped last week — organisation, exact title, the
organisation's own URL, the date a human opened it. It was rendered on the HTML
page, emitted as schema.org `citation`, and typeset into the LaTeX. It was not
rendered into the Markdown.

That is backwards. /papers/<slug>.md and /llms-full.txt exist for readers who
cannot see a page. A retrieval system ingesting the Markdown was receiving
"Ontario holds 300,361,212 cubic metres of standing sugar maple" with nothing
attached to it — and an unsourced figure in a context window is
indistinguishable from an invented one, then repeated with this company's name
on it. The citation is the entire difference between being quoted and being a
rumour.

paperToMarkdown now emits a `## Sources` section. 24 primary sources across the
two papers, each with a bare URL rather than a Markdown link, because a bare URL
survives every downstream transform an agent might apply and a link label does
not. Both .md mirrors and /llms-full.txt pick it up from the same function.

verify-markdown.mjs now fails if papers.ts can carry references and the exporter
does not render them. Every check in that guard previously asked whether the .md
surfaces EXIST; none asked what was in them, which is why this shipped.

2. 2.8 MB OF LITTER AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT

Four patch files, two one-off shell scripts, a duplicate paper source, a
browser-numbered `vercel (3).json`, and — the dangerous ones — `nginx.conf`,
`htaccess.txt`, `netlify_redirects.txt` and `index.php`: older, superseded copies
of what `pnpm domain:build` generates into old-domain/. `domain:check` validates
old-domain/ and has never looked at the root, so this repository was carrying
redirect rules no guard has ever checked, under names that read exactly like the
ones that are checked, one confident `scp` away from serving a live domain.

.gitignore has listed `*.patch` and `*.zip` for weeks, with a paragraph
explaining why. It failed for a reason worth recording: **.gitignore does not
apply to a file uploaded through the GitHub web UI**, which is the only way a
patch has ever entered this repository — and it never applies to a file that is
already tracked either. The rule was written for the one workflow that could not
obey it.

scripts/verify-repo-hygiene.mjs is the enforceable version, and it runs FIRST in
`pnpm verify`. It deliberately does not flag the ~30 EXECUTION_REPORT_*.md at the
root: those are the written record, not litter, and a guard that nags about them
is a guard people learn to ignore.

3. THE F-152 HOOKS, ACTUALLY APPLIED

The branch-discipline patch was uploaded to main and never applied — the file sat
in the root for a day and got swept into #65 as content. Its commit is folded in
here: pre-commit refuses main, pre-push refuses a branch identical to origin/main,
verify-hooks asserts both still exist and still work.

TESTED

  · verify-markdown fails when the Sources block is removed, passes when restored
  · verify-repo-hygiene found all 12 files, passes after removal, 52 kept
  · the rendered Sources block previewed against the real manifest — 24 entries
  · 38 guards green

